# Phase 2 Follow-Up Interview Transcript: Medicine & Technology Usage

**Participant:** Subrata Kumar Das  
**Age:** 64 years old  
**Gender:** Male  
**Background:** Retired AGM from Sonali Bank

---

## 1. Affiliation Ledger & Negotiating Allegiance

**Interviewer:** "After using the medicine reminder system, how did you feel about who the system was helping?"

**Subrata Kumar Das:** "It is mainly helping me because I manage my own medicines. I don't require anyone's help normally. So I see it as a support for my own routine."

**Interviewer:** "Did you feel it was clear about what it was doing, or did it feel like it was getting too involved?"

**Subrata Kumar Das:** "It was clear. If it is only reminding me about the medicine, then that is fine. I don't think it needs to interfere beyond that."

**Interviewer:** "Did you ever feel the need to involve someone else in using it?"

**Subrata Kumar Das:** "Not normally. I am comfortable with phones and computers, so I can manage it myself. If there is some important issue, then of course I can discuss it with the doctor."

**Interviewer:** "Would you prefer the system to work independently or together with you?"

**Subrata Kumar Das:** "It can work independently for reminders. I should remain in control. The system should remind me, not make medical decisions for me."

---

## 2. Veto Power, Risk, and Silence

**Interviewer:** "If the system gave you a reminder that did not match your prescription or the medicine package, what would you do?"

**Subrata Kumar Das:** "I would check the prescription and the package. I would not blindly follow the system. The hard copy prescription is still important."

**Interviewer:** "What if it gave a stronger warning because it thought there was a risk?"

**Subrata Kumar Das:** "If there is a genuine risk, then a stronger warning is useful. But it should be based on correct information. Medicine is a serious matter."

**Interviewer:** "If you did not respond to a reminder, what should the system do?"

**Subrata Kumar Das:** "It can remind me again after some time. Sometimes a person can be occupied with something else. But I would not want repeated alarms continuously."

**Interviewer:** "Would you want another person to be notified if you did not respond?"

**Subrata Kumar Das:** "For me, not necessarily. I manage my own medicines and I usually do not miss doses. If there is some unusual situation, then I would rather handle it myself or contact the doctor."

**Interviewer:** "How would you distinguish a normal reminder from a serious problem?"

**Subrata Kumar Das:** "A normal reminder is simply telling me it is time for the medicine. A serious problem would be something like a medicine being changed, an unexpected issue, or something that does not match the prescription. That should be treated separately."

---

## 3. Care Collectivism & "Checking as Love"

**Interviewer:** "Has the system changed how other people check on your medication?"

**Subrata Kumar Das:** "There was not much checking before because I already manage it myself. So the main change is that I have another reminder. It is not replacing any family routine."

**Interviewer:** "Do you feel that the reminder replaces the need to remember the medicine yourself?"

**Subrata Kumar Das:** "Not completely. My memory is still good. But having a reminder is useful as an additional support. There is no harm in having another check."

**Interviewer:** "If there was a sudden change in your medicine, would you let the system handle it?"

**Subrata Kumar Das:** "No. I would contact the doctor. My doctor reviews my prescriptions every three months, so any change should come from the doctor."

**Interviewer:** "Would you discuss a major medicine change with someone else?"

**Subrata Kumar Das:** "The important person would be the doctor. I would not make a change just because a software system suggested it."

---

## 4. Gamification as a Relational Trigger

**Interviewer:** "If the system showed a high score or a long streak for taking your medicines properly, would that be useful to you?"

**Subrata Kumar Das:** "It could show that I have been following the routine properly. But I don't think I need a score. Taking medicine on time is already my first priority."

**Interviewer:** "Would praise or encouragement from the system make a difference?"

**Subrata Kumar Das:** "It may be pleasant, but it is not necessary. I already take the medicines regularly."

**Interviewer:** "What if you missed a dose and the streak was broken?"

**Subrata Kumar Das:** "I would be more concerned about why I missed the dose than about the streak. The actual medicine is important, not the number."

**Interviewer:** "Would you want this progress to be shared with anyone?"

**Subrata Kumar Das:** "No particular need. Since I manage my own medication, I don't think the progress needs to be shared unless there is some actual problem."

---

## 5. Probationary Trust & Retraining

**Interviewer:** "When you first started using the system, did you feel you needed to test it before trusting it?"

**Subrata Kumar Das:** "Yes, initially I would compare it with my prescription and medicine packages. I would want to see whether the reminders are coming at the correct times."

**Interviewer:** "What would make you trust it?"

**Subrata Kumar Das:** "If the reminders consistently match the prescription, then I can depend on it more. But I would still keep the prescription because that is the proper reference."

**Interviewer:** "Did the system replace your memory of your medication schedule?"

**Subrata Kumar Das:** "No. My memory is suitable for me. The system is just another aid. It makes the routine a little safer because there is an additional reminder."

**Interviewer:** "Would you still want to review your prescription?"

**Subrata Kumar Das:** "Yes. Especially because my doctor reviews it every three months. If something changes, the reminder system also needs to be updated."

---

## 6. Closing & Plural Principal

**Interviewer:** "After using the system, who do you think it ultimately works for—you or your doctor and family as well?"

**Subrata Kumar Das:** "Primarily me, because I take care of my own medicines. The doctor is responsible for prescribing and reviewing them. The system is only supporting the routine."

**Interviewer:** "Would you be comfortable with the system becoming a family advocate if there was a problem?"

**Subrata Kumar Das:** "If there was a serious problem, some notification could be useful. But I don't think family needs to be involved in ordinary reminders because I can manage those myself."

**Interviewer:** "When would it be acceptable for the system to involve someone else?"

**Subrata Kumar Das:** "If there is something unusual that I cannot handle, or if there is a serious issue with the medicine, then it could be useful to involve someone. But normal medicine reminders should remain with me."

**Interviewer:** "Overall, has the system made your medicine routine easier?"

**Subrata Kumar Das:** "Yes. I did not have a major problem before because I remember my medicines well. But a reminder system is useful in this age of technology. It is an additional support."

**Interviewer:** "Would you recommend such a system for people your age?"

**Subrata Kumar Das:** "Yes, but it should be usable by everyone. Especially people who are not equally educated. It should not assume that everyone understands complicated software."

**Interviewer:** "What should the system do to make it easier for different people?"

**Subrata Kumar Das:** "It should be simple. The reminder should be clear, and the information should be easy to understand. Different people have different levels of education and technology experience, so that should be considered."

**Interviewer:** "Anything else you would like to add?"

**Subrata Kumar Das:** "No, that is all. The main thing is that the system should give correct reminders and be easy for people to use."

**Interviewer:** "Thank you for your time."

**Subrata Kumar Das:** "Welcome."
